# Introduction

Alternative mating strategies allow males to achieve reproductive success through divergent solutions to competition for mates. In many species, large or well-armed males defend females or reproductive resources, whereas smaller males avoid direct contests and obtain matings through sneaking, satellite behaviour or active searching (Gross 1996; Shuster & Wade 2003; Oliveira et al. 2008). These strategies are frequently associated with conspicuous differences in body size and weaponry, but alternative male phenotypes can differ in many additional aspects of morphology, physiology, behaviour and life history. They are therefore better understood as integrated phenotypes than as otherwise similar males differing only in the exaggerated trait used to classify them (West-Eberhard 2003). Selection should favour coordinated combinations of traits that facilitate the behaviour and ecological circumstances associated with each strategy.

Such phenotypic integration may arise because the development of one structure affects the resources available to others or because selection favours the coordinated expression of functionally complementary traits. Exaggerated sexually selected structures can impose developmental or performance costs that are mitigated by corresponding changes in supporting structures—a process termed secondary sexual trait compensation (Tomkins et al. 2005; Husak & Swallow 2011). Alternatively, investment in an exaggerated structure may occur at the expense of other traits when their development competes for a limited pool of resources (Nijhout & Emlen 1998; Emlen 2001; Moczek & Nijhout 2004). A third possibility is that investment in weapons and investment in supporting structures are positively integrated, such that individuals or morphs with larger weapons also invest more in locomotory and sensory traits—an outcome expected when overall phenotypic quality or developmental resources simultaneously elevate multiple traits (Bonduriansky & Day 2003; Tomkins et al. 2005). These processes need not be uniform among male morphs. Developmental switches underlying alternative phenotypes can alter the covariance among traits, producing morph-specific patterns of integration even though the morphs possess the same basic anatomical structures (Snell-Rood et al. 2010; Rohner et al. 2020).

Most work on morph-specific integration has focused on weapons and the structures that support their use. In horned dung beetles, for example, horn length is positively integrated with fore- and hindleg morphology, and this integration can be stronger in major than in minor males (Tomkins et al. 2005; Rohner et al. 2020), consistent with positive co-expression of weapons and supporting structures in the conventional morph. Similarly, male rhinoceros beetles with relatively long horns also have relatively long wings and foretibiae, potentially improving locomotion and leverage during combat, although horn size is unrelated to eye size after accounting for body size (McCullough & Emlen 2013). By contrast, in the male-dimorphic stag beetle *Prosopocoilus inclinatus*, weapon size is integrated with eye size in major males but with foreleg length in minor males (Ito et al. 2017), suggesting that the traits co-expressed with weaponry can differ between morphs rather than being uniformly larger in the morph with the largest weapons. These findings show that weapon exaggeration can be accompanied by coordinated changes in other traits, but that the pattern and direction of integration need not be predictable from weapon size alone.

Locomotor and sensory traits should be especially relevant to alternative mating strategies because males must both locate mating opportunities and gain access to them. The spatial distribution of receptive females can generate a fundamental contrast between remaining with and defending current mates and searching for additional mates (Shuster & Wade 2003). Males that patrol widely or move among defended mating sites may consequently benefit from locomotor structures that enhance movement, whereas males that defend females may benefit from appendages that provide support or leverage during combat. Sensory demands may also differ among strategies, though the direction of morph differences is not predetermined. In the desert bee *Centris pallida*, small males rely primarily on vision for mate detection while large males use chemosensory cues; correspondingly, small males possess relatively larger eyes despite their smaller body size (Barrett et al. 2021; Barrett & O’Donnell 2026), consistent with enhanced sensory investment in the morph that actively searches for mates. However, if conventional males require accurate sensory assessment of rivals or of gallery quality to defend harems effectively, the direction of morph differences in sensory investment could be reversed. Few studies have examined locomotor and sensory traits together within a single species to determine whether such differences represent coordinated phenotypic divergence or trait-specific responses to the demands of each strategy.

The Wellington tree wētā, *Hemideina crassidens*, provides an opportunity to address this question. This flightless, nocturnal orthopteran is harem-polygynous, and females shelter communally in cavities in trees, hereafter galleries. Males use their enlarged heads and mandibles as weapons in contests for control of female-occupied galleries, and males with larger weapons generally have an advantage during escalated fights (Kelly 2005, 2006; Kelly & Adams 2010). Males express three genetically polymorphic, irreversible morphotypes through maturation at the eighth, ninth or tenth juvenile instar (Nason & Kelly 2020; Kelly 2024). Large tenth-instar males possess the most exaggerated weaponry and commonly fight for control of galleries. Small eighth-instar males possess much smaller weapons and may circumvent larger defenders to obtain copulations or search vegetation for undefended females, whereas ninth-instar males exhibit an intermediate strategy that combines fighting and sneaking depending on the social context (Wilson & Kelly 2019; Kelly 2025). The three strategies attain similar average reproductive fitness, consistent with their maintenance as a balanced polymorphism (Nason & Kelly 2020).

Here, I tested whether these alternative male morphotypes differ in the relative expression and allometric scaling of locomotor and sensory traits. I measured the lengths of the fore-, mid- and hindlegs as indices of locomotor morphology, and I measured eye size and the area of the tympanal ears on the foretibiae as indices of visual and auditory investment. Females were included as a non-morphotypic reference group that shares the species’ general locomotor and sensory requirements but does not use enlarged mandibles in competition for mates. I asked whether the three leg pairs show a common pattern of allocation among groups or whether particular groups disproportionately invest in specific legs, and whether any anterior-posterior shift in allocation accompanies strategy divergence. I also tested whether weapon size is positively integrated with, negatively associated with, or independent of locomotory and sensory investment within groups—a comparison that directly distinguishes positive integration from resource trade-offs and developmental decoupling.

To my knowledge, this study is the first to examine locomotory and sensory morphology jointly across females and every male morphotype in a trimorphic insect expressing genetically polymorphic alternative mating strategies. Previous studies have generally considered a limited set of weapon-associated traits, often in male-dimorphic systems, and have rarely combined body-size-adjusted comparisons with analyses of allometric scaling and within-morph covariance. By integrating body-size-adjusted trait comparisons, group-specific allometry, anterior–posterior leg allocation, local anatomical integration and within-group weapon–trait covariance, my framework distinguishes morphological divergence among strategies from developmental integration within them. Because morph identity in *H. crassidens* is genetically polymorphic rather than induced by nutritional condition, comparisons among morphs are also less confounded by condition-dependent morph expression than in many polyphenic systems. This multilevel approach allows me to test whether alternative strategies represent broadly coordinated phenotypes or mosaics of trait-specific modification.

Two competing outcomes are possible. Under a searching and detection hypothesis, eighth-instar males—which locate mates by patrolling vegetation and circumventing gallery defenders—should face greater locomotory and sensory demands than their gallery-defending counterparts. If morphology tracks these demands, eighth-instar males should possess relatively longer legs and larger sensory structures at a common body size, and weapon size within groups should be decoupled from or negatively associated with locomotory and sensory investment. Under a positive-integration hypothesis, the morphotypic differences in weapon size reflect broader variation in developmental resources or overall phenotypic quality, such that conventional tenth-instar males simultaneously express larger weapons, longer legs, and greater sensory investment. On this view, weapon size within groups should be positively integrated with supporting structures regardless of morph identity. These two hypotheses generate opposite predictions for which morph leads in relative locomotory and sensory investment, and for the sign of within-group weapon–trait covariance. Ninth-instar males were expected to be intermediate or more generalized, consistent with their flexible combination of fighting and sneaking. More broadly, group-specific allometric slopes would indicate that trait expression scales differently with body size among morphotypes, whereas differences in elevation with common slopes would indicate proportional shifts in relative investment. By testing these predictions simultaneously across locomotory and sensory traits, I ask whether the alternative mating strategies of H. crassidens are expressed as broadly integrated phenotypes or as a mosaic of trait-specific adjustments.

# Methods

## Study site and specimen collection

Adult Wellington tree wētā, *Hemideina crassidens*, were haphazardly collected at night from vegetation on Te Pākeka/Maud Island, New Zealand (41°02′ S, 173°54′ E), during March and April of 2011 ($n = 74$), 2015 ($n = 123$) and 2016 ($n = 107$). Te Pākeka/Maud Island is a 309-ha scientific reserve in the Marlborough Sounds that is free of introduced mammalian predators. The sample collected in each year included females and males representing each of the three adult male morphotypes.

Adult males were assigned to eighth-, ninth- or tenth-instar morphotypes from their head lengths using the morph-specific distribution parameters established for this population by Kelly [-@Kelly2025]. These parameters were derived from a trimodal mixture model of male head length [@Kelly2025].

Following collection, individuals were euthanized by freezing at −20 °C. Each specimen was subsequently dissected to remove the head, pronotum, both forelegs, both midlegs and both hindlegs. All structures dissected from an individual were placed together in a labelled screw-cap tube to preserve their association with the individual's unique identifier.

