## Weapon–leg integration

To test whether relative weapon investment was positively integrated with leg size across groups—as predicted if weapon and locomotory traits are co-expressed rather than traded off—I extended the joint leg model to include relative head size (weapon size adjusted for pronotum length) as an additional covariate. The association was consistently positive: `r hl_n_positive` of `r hl_n_total` group-by-leg combinations had 95% confidence intervals entirely above zero, and no estimated slope was negative. The three-way interaction of group, leg, and relative head size was significant (`r fmt_f_anova(hl_int_row)`), indicating that the magnitude of weapon–leg integration varied across leg pairs and groups. The planned strategy slope contrasts, however, were `r ifelse(hl_strat_all_ns, "uniformly non-significant", "largely non-significant")` after Holm adjustment, meaning that no morph showed a weapon–leg integration profile that clearly differed from the others in the planned comparisons. Taken together, these results are consistent with positive phenotypic integration between weapon and leg investment, with no evidence of a negative trade-off.

## Sensory morphology

### Group differences at a common body size

Ear size (tympanic organ area) and eye length both differed significantly among groups at a common pronotum length. Allometric slopes for ear size on pronotum length did not differ among groups (`r fmt_f_nested(ear_body_test)`), nor did those for eye length (`r fmt_f_nested(eye_body_test)`), justifying a common-slope model for body-size adjustment in both cases.

At a pronotum length of `r fmt_num(common_body_size$reference_pronotum, 1)` mm, tenth-instar males had tympanic organ areas that were `r fmt_num(ear_body_strat_tenth_female$area_percent_difference, 1)`% larger in area than females (Holm-adjusted `r fmt_p(ear_body_strat_tenth_female$p.value)`), while eighth-instar males had areas `r fmt_num(abs(ear_body_strat_eighth_tenth$area_percent_difference), 1)`% smaller than tenth-instar males (Holm-adjusted `r fmt_p(ear_body_strat_eighth_tenth$p.value)`). The ninth-instar intermediacy contrast was not significant (`r fmt_p(ear_body_strat_ninth_mid$p.value)`). Eye length showed a larger and more pronounced gradient: tenth-instar males had eyes `r fmt_num(eye_body_strat_tenth_female$percent_difference, 1)`% longer than females (Holm-adjusted `r fmt_p(eye_body_strat_tenth_female$p.value)`), while eighth-instar males had eyes `r fmt_num(abs(eye_body_strat_eighth_tenth$percent_difference), 1)`% shorter than tenth-instar males (Holm-adjusted `r fmt_p(eye_body_strat_eighth_tenth$p.value)`). Again, ninth-instar males were statistically intermediate (`r fmt_p(eye_body_strat_ninth_mid$p.value)`). In both sensory traits, the morph gradient—from eighth-instar to tenth-instar—paralleled the weapon-size gradient, with conventional tenth-instar males investing most in both sensory structures and sneak-mating eighth-instar males investing least.

### Local morphological integration

I tested whether ear and eye size were locally integrated with the morphological structures they are most closely associated with—the foretibia (for the tympanic organ) and the head capsule (for compound eyes)—after removing group-specific body-size variation from both the sensory and predictor traits. Local integration slopes did not differ significantly among groups for either the ear–foretibia relationship (`r fmt_f_nested(ear_local_test)`) or the eye–head relationship (`r fmt_f_nested(eye_local_test)`). The 95% confidence interval was entirely above zero for `r ear_local_n_positive` of `r nrow(ear_local_slopes)` ear–foretibia slopes and `r eye_local_n_positive` of `r nrow(eye_local_slopes)` eye–head slopes, indicating broadly consistent local developmental coupling. The absence of supported group differences in these slopes suggests that the ear–foretibia and eye–head integration patterns are similar across the three mating strategies and females.

The positive eye-head slope deserves particular interpretive attention: because the head capsule is the same structure that bears the mandibular weaponry, weapon size and eye size are necessarily measured on the same structure. Individuals with proportionally larger heads will tend to have larger eyes partly as a geometric consequence of head capsule size, independently of any allocation decision. The eye-head integration slopes (`r fmt_num(min(eye_local_slopes[[eye_local_trend_col]]), 2)`–`r fmt_num(max(eye_local_slopes[[eye_local_trend_col]]), 2)` across groups) should therefore be interpreted as reflecting structural rather than necessarily adaptive coupling between vision and weapon investment.

### Weapon–sensory covariance

To directly test whether sensory investment was positively integrated with, traded off against, or decoupled from weapon investment, I regressed ear size on relative head size (weapon proxy, adjusted for body size) within each group. The group × weapon interaction for ear size was not significant (`r fmt_f_nested(ear_weapon_test)`), indicating a common relationship between weapon investment and ear investment across groups. The female weapon-ear slope was positive and clearly different from zero (`r fmt_num(ear_weapon_female[[ear_weapon_trend_col]], 3)`, 95% CI `r fmt_num(ear_weapon_female$lower.CL, 3)`–`r fmt_num(ear_weapon_female$upper.CL, 3)`, `r fmt_p(ear_weapon_female$p.value)`). Among the three male morphs, however, weapon-ear slopes ranged from `r fmt_num(min(ear_weapon_slopes[[ear_weapon_trend_col]][ear_weapon_slopes$group != "female"]), 3)` to `r fmt_num(max(ear_weapon_slopes[[ear_weapon_trend_col]][ear_weapon_slopes$group != "female"]), 3)` and none was individually significant at α = `r alpha` (all p ≥ `r fmt_num(min(ear_weapon_slopes$p.value[ear_weapon_slopes$group != "female"]), 3)`). This pattern—positive covariance in females, near-zero covariance in males—is consistent with developmental decoupling of ear investment from weapon investment within male morphs. There was no evidence of a trade-off (negative slope) in any group.

## Model diagnostics

Observed and model-predicted trait distributions corresponded closely. Residuals were approximately normally distributed and showed broadly homogeneous variance, with only minor curvature near the extremes of the fitted ranges. Collinearity remained below conventional concern thresholds, and no observation showed sufficient influence to justify exclusion after the underlying measurements had been checked. The log–log linear models and the repeated-trait mixed model were therefore retained for inference.

# Discussion

Alternative mating strategies are expected to comprise coordinated combinations of traits that support different routes to reproductive success. My results provide qualified support for this view but show that morphological coordination in Wellington tree wētā is more complex than either of my original hypotheses predicted. The searching and detection hypothesis was not supported: at a common body size, eighth-instar males did not possess the longest legs or the largest sensory structures. Instead, relative leg length generally increased from eighth- to tenth-instar males, and the same morph gradient was especially clear for eye length and tympanic organ area. The positive-integration hypothesis received stronger support because weapon size covaried positively with leg length within nearly every group, with no evidence that exaggerated head size was associated with reduced locomotory morphology. However, this integration did not extend uniformly to sensory traits. Ear investment differed substantially among morphs but was only weakly related to relative weapon size within male morphs, while local ear–foretibia and eye–head integration was broadly conserved across groups. Alternative mating strategies in this species therefore appear to be expressed as a mosaic of trait-specific changes rather than as uniformly integrated whole-organism phenotypes.

## Locomotory divergence among mating strategies

The three male morphs formed a consistent gradient in size-adjusted leg length, with tenth-instar males possessing the longest fore-, mid- and hindlegs and eighth-instar males the shortest. Ninth-instar males were generally intermediate and statistically indistinguishable from the midpoint between the two other male morphs. This morphological intermediacy is consistent with their proposed jack-of-all-trades strategy, in which males can either fight for galleries or circumvent larger competitors depending on the social context (Wilson & Kelly 2019; Kelly 2025). It does not, however, indicate that ninth-instar morphology is specifically adapted for behavioural flexibility. An intermediate phenotype could also arise directly from maturation at an intermediate instar. Functional tests are therefore needed to establish whether the morphological position of ninth-instar males enhances their ability to alternate between fighting and searching tactics.

Comparisons with females revealed a pattern that was not apparent from comparisons among male morphs alone. All male groups had relatively longer forelegs than females, whereas male–female differences in hindleg length varied from negative in eighth-instar males to positive in tenth-instar males. Consequently, tenth-instar males displayed a pronounced anterior shift in leg allocation relative to females. The anterior legs may contribute to stabilizing the body, maintaining purchase on the substrate or manoeuvring during encounters involving the head and mandibles. Such functions would make disproportionate anterior investment particularly valuable to heavily armed, gallery-defending males. Similar reasoning has been applied to the association between horns and foreleg morphology in dung and rhinoceros beetles, where the legs can provide leverage or support during contests (Tomkins et al. 2005; McCullough & Emlen 2013). Nevertheless, the present data describe morphology rather than mechanical performance, and the proposed role of anterior legs in combat remains to be tested directly.

The absence of comparable anterior–posterior allocation differences among the three male morphs is also informative. Eighth- and tenth-instar males differ markedly in weapon size and reproductive behaviour, yet they did not exhibit strongly divergent foreleg-to-hindleg allocation after body size was controlled. The primary locomotory distinction among male morphs was therefore a general gradient in the lengths of all three leg pairs rather than a major redistribution of investment from posterior to anterior appendages. A simple distinction between long-legged searching males and anteriorly specialized fighting males is consequently not supported. Instead, the clearest shift in relative leg allocation appears to separate heavily armed tenth-instar males from females, rather than separating conventional and unconventional males.

All leg traits scaled hypoallometrically with pronotum length, meaning that larger individuals within each group possessed proportionally shorter legs. Tenth-instar males generally had the shallowest estimated slopes, although support for specific slope differences was limited primarily to the hindleg comparison between females and tenth-instar males. Thus, the leg differences among groups were driven more consistently by changes in trait elevation at a common body size than by wholesale reprogramming of allometric slopes. This contrasts with weapon allometry in the same population, which differs substantially among male morphs, with eighth-instar weapons scaling more steeply than those of ninth- and tenth-instar males (Kelly 2025). Locomotory and weapon morphology therefore need not share the same morph-specific scaling rules, further supporting a modular or mosaic interpretation of morphological divergence.

## Positive weapon–leg integration

Within groups, individuals possessing relatively larger heads generally also possessed relatively longer legs. Eleven of the 12 group-by-leg slopes were clearly positive, and none was negative. These results provide no evidence for the hypothesis that greater investment in weaponry is realized at the expense of locomotory appendages. Instead, they are consistent with positive phenotypic integration between weapons and structures that may support their use. This pattern resembles secondary sexual trait compensation, in which enlargement of a sexual trait is accompanied by enlargement of structures that bear, manoeuvre or otherwise improve the performance of that trait (Tomkins et al. 2005; Husak & Swallow 2011).

The present results also resemble those reported for the European earwig, another hemimetabolous insect in which male morphs differ in weapon size. Tomkins et al. (2005) found positive integration between forceps length and hind-femur length in exaggerated macrolabic males, but no evidence that forceps enlargement traded off against the other measured traits. They proposed that morphological trade-offs may be less readily expressed in hemimetabolous insects than in holometabolous taxa because growth is not confined to a closed pupal developmental system. Wellington tree wētā likewise acquire resources and grow through successive nymphal instars, and the uniformly non-negative weapon–leg relationships are consistent with that proposal. This comparison remains tentative, however, because the absence of a negative phenotypic correlation does not demonstrate the absence of an underlying allocation cost. Variation among individuals in resource acquisition can produce positive covariance among costly traits even when those traits compete for resources during development.

Unlike the morph-specific integration observed in European earwigs, the planned comparisons in Wellington tree wētā did not show that weapon–leg integration was consistently stronger in tenth-instar males than in the unconventional morphs. Although the overall group-by-leg-by-weapon interaction indicated heterogeneity among slopes, no planned morph contrast remained clearly supported after correction for multiple comparisons. The positive relationship may therefore reflect a shared developmental architecture linking head and appendage growth rather than a compensatory programme uniquely expressed by heavily armed males. Alternatively, similar slopes could be maintained by correlational selection in all groups because legs contribute to functions other than combat, including climbing, foraging, predator avoidance and mate searching. The evidence is consequently best described as positive phenotypic integration consistent with secondary sexual trait compensation, rather than as proof that longer legs evolved specifically to offset the mechanical costs of larger weapons.

## Sensory divergence among morphs

Sensory morphology showed an especially clear morph gradient. At a common pronotum length, tenth-instar males had the largest eyes and tympanic organs, eighth-instar males had the smallest, and ninth-instar males were intermediate. This pattern is opposite to the prediction that the actively searching eighth-instar morph would invest most heavily in sensory structures used to locate mating opportunities. It also contrasts with the pattern in the desert bee *Centris pallida*, in which small searching males rely more strongly on visual detection and possess relatively larger eyes than large males (Barrett et al. 2021; Barrett & O’Donnell 2026). Sensory investment therefore cannot be predicted simply from whether a morph searches actively or defends access to females.

The greater sensory dimensions of tenth-instar males could reflect demands associated with gallery defence, rival assessment or navigation between refuges and mating opportunities. Alternatively, sensory enlargement may form part of a broader developmental programme associated with delayed maturation and increased weapon expression, without having evolved specifically for the tenth-instar mating strategy. The common sensory allometric slopes favour the latter possibility in at least one respect. Morph differences in eye and ear size reflected shifts in elevation rather than differences in how sensory dimensions increased with body size. Thus, each group followed approximately the same scaling relationship but at a different relative level of sensory expression. Similar elevation shifts can generate substantial sexual or morph-specific exaggeration without the evolution of steeper allometric slopes, as recently demonstrated for elongated sensory antennae in crane flies (Hirani & Bonduriansky 2026).

Morphological dimensions alone cannot establish whether these differences translate into sensory performance. Longer eyes might contain more ommatidia, provide greater sensitivity under low light or simply reflect enlargement of the head capsule. Similarly, a larger tympanic membrane could affect auditory sensitivity or frequency response, but membrane area alone does not quantify hearing performance. The present measures are therefore best interpreted as indices of morphological investment rather than direct measures of visual or auditory ability. Physiological measurements of auditory thresholds, eye structure and behavioural responses to visual or acoustic stimuli will be required to determine the functional significance of the observed morph differences.

## Local integration and sensory decoupling

Both sensory structures were positively related to the anatomical structures on which they occur. Individuals with relatively larger foretibiae possessed larger tympanic organs, and individuals with relatively larger heads possessed larger eyes. Neither local relationship differed clearly among females and the three male morphs. These results suggest that ear–foretibia and eye–head covariance forms part of a conserved anatomical or developmental architecture that is largely unaffected by male strategy expression. Such local coupling is unsurprising in the case of the eye because eye dimensions are physically constrained by the size and geometry of the head capsule. The positive eye–head relationship should therefore not be taken as evidence that selection has adaptively coordinated visual investment with weapon enlargement. Some portion of the association is likely to arise mechanically or developmentally because the eye and mandibular apparatus occupy the same structure.

The relationship between weapon and ear investment provides a useful contrast. Tympanic organ area differed substantially among morphs, yet within-morph weapon–ear slopes were modest and none of the three male slopes was individually different from zero. This suggests that the developmental processes generating among-morph differences in ear size are at least partly independent of those generating variation in weapon size among males within the same morph. However, the group-by-relative-head interaction was not significant, so the fact that the female slope was individually significant whereas the male slopes were not cannot itself be interpreted as a supported difference between females and males. The conservative conclusion is that there was no evidence of a weapon–ear trade-off and limited evidence for strong within-morph integration in males.

This distinction between among-group divergence and within-group covariance is central to interpreting integrated phenotypes. Traits can differ in parallel among morph means without being tightly integrated among individuals within each morph. Conversely, traits can covary strongly within groups even when the magnitude of that covariance does not differ among strategies. Recent work on an exaggerated male sensory trait likewise found that substantial sexual divergence can be accompanied by developmental decoupling from the rest of the phenotype (Hirani & Bonduriansky 2026). Such decoupling may facilitate independent evolutionary change by reducing the extent to which enlargement of one structure alters others. In Wellington tree wētā, sensory traits appear to retain strong coupling with their immediate anatomical substrates while being more weakly connected to variation in weapon investment. Morphological integration is therefore hierarchical: strong locally, positive between weapons and legs, but comparatively weak between weapons and ears within male morphs.

## The value of females as a reference

Including females revealed features of male morphology that would have been overlooked in a male-only analysis. The strongest anterior shift in locomotory allocation involved tenth-instar males relative to females, whereas allocation differences among the male morphs were comparatively small. Females also had smaller eyes than all three male morphs and smaller tympanic organs than ninth- and tenth-instar males at the common body size. These comparisons suggest that some components of the observed morphological gradient reflect male-specific divergence rather than merely the addition of successive instars.

Females should not, however, be treated as an unselected control or as an estimate of the naturally selected optimum. Female legs and sensory structures are subject to their own combination of selection pressures associated with foraging, predator avoidance, reproduction and refuge use. Moreover, females mature at the tenth instar but do not express the exaggerated male head and mandibles. Their inclusion is valuable because they share the general body plan and ecology of males while lacking the same form of weapon-mediated sexual competition, not because their morphology represents a neutral developmental baseline.

## Developmental interpretation of a genetically polymorphic system

The genetically polymorphic expression of the three male strategies strengthens the interpretation of among-morph differences. In many insects with alternative male phenotypes, morph identity depends on nutritional condition, making it difficult to distinguish trait specialization from broad differences in resource acquisition. Wellington tree wētā males raised under common conditions nevertheless mature at different instars and express the corresponding weapon morphologies, indicating that morph identity is not simply induced by poor or favourable nutrition (Kelly 2024). The leg and sensory differences observed here are therefore less readily explained as incidental consequences of condition-dependent morph determination.

Genetic control of morph identity does not mean that all morphological divergence is adaptive or environmentally insensitive. Field-collected individuals inevitably differ in developmental conditions, and variation in food, temperature or health could affect the final sizes of weapons, legs and sensory structures within each genetically influenced morph. Moreover, the morphs differ in the number of instars completed before maturity. Some of the morphological gradient may therefore result from heterochrony or from the cumulative developmental effects of additional moults rather than from independent selection on each trait. Body-size adjustment removes the most obvious effect of overall size, but it cannot eliminate developmental-history differences between individuals that reached the same pronotum length through different maturation pathways. Common-garden manipulations that follow multiple traits across successive instars would help separate tactic-specific allocation from general consequences of delayed maturation.

## Limitations and future directions

Several limitations define the scope of inference. First, this study is based on adult morphology and cannot identify the developmental mechanism producing trait covariance. Positive weapon–leg integration might arise through shared hormonal regulation, pleiotropy, genetic covariance, correlated selection or variation in resource acquisition. Second, head size is a biologically relevant proxy for weapon investment but is also associated with feeding and provides the substrate on which the eyes develop. Relationships involving relative head size therefore need not arise solely from selection on the mandibles as weapons. Third, the study did not measure locomotory, fighting or sensory performance. The adaptive value of longer legs, larger eyes and larger tympanic organs remains hypothetical until morphology is linked to functional outcomes. Finally, tenth-instar males were less numerous than the other groups, reducing precision for some morph-specific slopes and making weak interaction effects difficult to interpret.

The most direct next step is to connect morphological allocation to performance and fitness. Locomotory assays could test whether leg dimensions predict climbing speed, stability or manoeuvrability and whether those relationships differ among strategies. Contest experiments could determine whether anterior leg dimensions improve fighting performance independently of weapon size. Auditory and visual assays could establish whether larger tympanic organs and eyes enhance sensory sensitivity under biologically relevant conditions. Developmental experiments could manipulate resource availability while following genetically assigned morphs across instars, thereby testing whether weapon–leg and local sensory integration are environmentally stable. Finally, estimates of genetic covariance and correlational selection would help distinguish shared developmental regulation from adaptive trait compensation.

## Conclusions

The alternative male strategies of Wellington tree wētā are not simply weapon morphs accompanied by uniformly scaled versions of the remaining phenotype. Relative leg and sensory dimensions generally increased along the eighth- to tenth-instar weapon gradient, but anterior–posterior leg allocation differed most clearly between tenth-instar males and females rather than among the male strategies. Weapon and leg investment were positively integrated within groups, providing no evidence of a simple morphological trade-off, whereas sensory structures retained strong local anatomical integration but were comparatively weakly associated with weapon variation within male morphs. These findings show that alternative mating phenotypes can combine coordinated divergence, conserved developmental relationships and partial trait decoupling within the same organism. Distinguishing differences among morph means from patterns of covariance within morphs is therefore essential for understanding how alternative mating strategies become embodied as integrated—or partially integrated—phenotypes.

